%`-

txt group 108 on
par4=1

func 3 "www www.math.utk.edu/~morwen/knotscape.html"
exit

%`+

The KnotPlot `diagram' command uses an auxilary program written by
Morwen Thistlethwaite and Kenneth Stephenson and part of the Knotscape
software package. KnotPlot's author would like to thank Morwen and Ken
for their kind permission to use their software within KnotPlot.

Clicking on the `diagram' button is equivalent to the KnotPlot command

diagram .

What this does is to compute the DT code and then send off this code to the
diagram engine for a new embedding.  If things work properly the new 
embedding should have the same DT code as the knot you started with (but 
see the note below).

A few things to note:

  --- the diagram engine works only for prime proper (one string) knots
      without any nugatory crossings (click on the ZWT button to see a
      knot with three nugatory crossings)

  --- in this demo, the DT codes are computed according to the current view
      projection.  this is usually a perspective projection and is different
      than the orthographic projection to the xy-plane.  however, the diagram
      is generated in such a way that the new embedding has the requested DT
      with respect to the orthographic projection.  since the displayed DT 
      code of the new embedding is still based on the perspective projection,
      the code may appear to change.  since the new embeddings are rather 
      flat, this generally doesn't happen. 


References:

Knotscape - http://www.math.utk.edu/~morwen/knotscape.html
            Morwen Thistlethwaite's Knotscape page, press F3 now to go there

